	<key>PayloadDescription</key>
	<string>Keeps the Family Site Blocker add-on installed and switched on in Firefox, for every account on this Mac. Firefox installs the newest signed version from https://adviseworn.github.io/family-site-blocker-updates/family-site-blocker.xpi, keeps it up to date, and nobody can turn it off or remove it there. Firefox also gets its own copy of the add-on’s built-in list of sites (whole adult domains such as .porn and .xxx, and the sites people visit most), which keeps blocking them when add-ons don’t run, as in Troubleshoot Mode. Private windows, Troubleshoot Mode from Firefox’s menus, about:config, Refresh Firefox and the developer tools, which could get round it, are turned off. Settings that other programs, such as another blocker, give Firefox for their own add-ons stay in force. This is the copy from version 1.15.0 of Family Site Blocker, with 1,000 entries in Firefox’s own list; the add-on’s settings page says whether that list or the lock’s other settings have changed since. Only an administrator can remove this profile.</string>
	<key>PayloadDisplayName</key>
	<string>Family Site Blocker for Firefox</string>
	<key>PayloadIdentifier</key>
	<string>local.parental-controls.family-site-blocker.firefox</string>
	<key>PayloadOrganization</key>
	<string>Family Site Blocker</string>
	<key>PayloadScope</key>
	<string>System</string>
	<key>PayloadType</key>
	<string>Configuration</string>
	<key>PayloadUUID</key>
	<string>C3B0C6B7-3BE0-4536-A299-E2F9BF95B6E2</string>
